    {
      "id": "claim_92ac606da061412cb7ff154df9626d48",
      "statement": "ctrtool's parse_cia and extract_files_from_cia deliberately bypass run_tool, calling Command::output() and checking status themselves so a non-zero exit is tolerated when stdout ends with \"[ctrtool::NcchProcess ERROR] NcchHeader is corrupted (Bad struct magic).\" (the expected result of -p on a non-homebrew title); routing them through run_tool would break retail CIA imports. They also use .expect on spawn instead of an anyhow context error.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/ctrtool.rs#L111-L132",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:cb2e47b37208d239e7d5a8f22c747ef0e331c6f84e27e2c4682d2bd2efcd064e:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/ctrtool.rs#L42-L58",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:647340884b22c13192eec3ec165647ff9fb93b17ddef100621d5aca5f64d9918:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3f33fd2b40e84bbf9e67b7374e45b36a",
      "statement": "ctrtool::extract_files_from_cia runs the tool with --certs/--tmd/--contents and current_dir set to the caller's temp directory, then treats stderr as data, deriving the list of extracted content paths from \"[ctrtool::CiaProcess LOG] Saving content \" lines.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/ctrtool.rs#L111-L139",
